        <!-- SECTION 1: Definition (THE ANCHOR) -->
        <p>The term <strong>Western fine art</strong> has historically been conflated with painting—oils on canvas depicting frontier mythology, cowboy stoicism, and romanticized landscapes. Today, the term appears across painting, photography, galleries, and museums—often without agreement on what qualifies. But the term itself makes no reference to medium. Western fine art is not a medium. It is an authorship standard. As a result, Western fine art now encompasses painting, photography, and other media—so long as the work meets the same authorship standard.</p>

        <p>As the art world continues to dissolve arbitrary boundaries between photography and painting, the definition of what qualifies as fine art in the Western genre has become both contested and essential to understand. This guide reconciles those definitions and clarifies why the distinction matters for collectors, curators, and anyone engaging seriously with the American West as subject matter.</p>

        <!-- EARLY FUNNEL -->
        <div class="early-funnel" style="background:#f8f6f3; border-left:4px solid #8b5a2b; padding:1.25rem 1.5rem; margin:2rem 0; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">
