        <!-- SECTION 5: What Western Fine Art Is Not (CONTRAST) -->
        <div class="section-heading-with-line">
          <h2>What Western Fine Art Is Not</h2>
        </div>

        <p>To understand what qualifies as Western fine art, it helps to clarify what does not:</p>

        <ul style="list-style:disc; padding-left:1.5rem; margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;">
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>Décor art.</strong> Work created primarily to match interior color schemes or fill wall space. Fine art may be displayed in homes, but it is not designed for that purpose.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>Tourist art.</strong> Imagery optimized for mass appeal, nostalgia, or souvenir value. This includes overly saturated sunsets, clichéd cowboy portraits, and generic landscape photography sold in gift shops.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>Documentary-only photography.</strong> While documentary work can be artistically significant, its primary function is record, not authorship. Fine art prioritizes vision and interpretation over journalistic accuracy or historical record.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>Genre clichés.</strong> Reliance on visual tropes—wagon wheels, weathered fences, boots and spurs—without compositional purpose or narrative depth. Fine art may include these elements, but never as shorthand.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The line is intent. If the work exists primarily to sell quickly, appeal broadly, or reinforce expectation, it is not fine art.</p>

        <p>Western fine art exists to be considered over time—returning meaning rather than immediate gratification.</p>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Why the Distinction Matters -->
        <div class="section-heading-with-line">
          <h2>Why the Distinction Matters</h2>
        </div>

        <p>For collectors, the distinction between Western fine art and Western-themed commercial work determines long-term value, both financially and culturally. Fine art appreciates. It becomes part of private and public collections. It is written about, exhibited, and preserved. Commercial work, by contrast, is replaceable.</p>

        <p>For curators and institutions, the ability to identify fine art within the Western genre allows for more sophisticated programming. It moves the conversation beyond "cowboy nostalgia" and into the realm of serious contemporary engagement with American identity, landscape, and history.</p>

        <p>And for artists working in this space, the distinction is existential. To be classified as fine art is to be taken seriously. It is to have your work considered alongside painting, sculpture, and other established forms. It is to participate in a tradition rather than be dismissed by it.</p>

        <p>The stakes are high because the term "Western fine art" is still contested. As photography continues to gain legitimacy, the artists and institutions that define its standards will shape how the next generation understands the American West—not as myth, but as living subject matter worthy of rigorous, uncompromising artistic treatment.</p>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Explore (SOFT CTA) -->
        <div class="section-heading-with-line">
